.title = "ZML API reference",
.description = "Call forms, return types, and ownership for Tensor, Shape, Buffer, Platform, and Exe.",
.date = .date("2026-11-17T00:00:00"),
.layout = "content.shtml",
---
These are the core calls for defining, compiling, and running a model at the
revision used in the [ZML quickstart]($link.page('zml/quickstart')). All five
types are exported by `@import("zml")`. The call forms below use a
`std.mem.Allocator` for `allocator` and `std.Io` for `io`. A return type with
`!` can carry an error.

# [Tensor]($heading.id('tensor'))

[`zml.Tensor`](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/tensor.zig)
represents a symbolic input, weight, or intermediate result. Use operations
such as `dot` inside the function ZML traces during compilation.

- `zml.Tensor.init(dimensions, dtype)`\
  `Tensor`. Declare a symbolic tensor from dimensions and a `zml.DataType` such as `.f32`.
- `zml.Tensor.fromShape(shape)`\
  `Tensor`. Declare a symbolic tensor from an existing `zml.Shape`.
- `tensor.shape()`\
  `Shape`. Read the tensor's shape metadata.
- `tensor.withTags(tags)`\
  `Tensor`. Return the tensor with one tag per axis, in axis order.
- `tensor.axis(axis)` / `tensor.dim(axis)`\
  `u3` index / `i64` size. Select an axis by index or tag.
- `lhs.dot(rhs, tags)`\
  `Tensor`. Contract the named axes in both operands. Their sizes and the operands' element types must match. Other shared tags become batch axes.

See the [named-axis example]($link.page('zml/concepts').ref('tagged-dot-product'))
for how contraction determines the result shape.

# [Shape]($heading.id('shape'))

[`zml.Shape`](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/shape.zig)
holds metadata including the element type, dimension sizes, and tags.

- `zml.Shape.init(dimensions, dtype)`\
  `Shape`. Use a tuple such as `.{ 2, 3 }` for sizes or named fields such as `.{ .output = 2, .feature = 3 }` to assign tags as well.
- `zml.Shape.scalar(dtype)`\
  `Shape`. Describe one element with zero axes.
- `shape.rank()` / `shape.dtype()`\
  `u4` number of axes / `DataType` element type.
- `shape.dim(axis)` / `shape.axis(axis)`\
  `i64` size / `u3` index. Accept a numeric axis or a tag.
- `shape.dims()`\
  `[]const i64`. Borrow the ordered sizes from the shape. Keep the shape alive while using the slice.
- `shape.count()`\
  `usize`. Return the number of elements.

# [Buffer]($heading.id('buffer'))

[`zml.Buffer`](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/buffer.zig)
holds backend handles for a concrete tensor. Create the inputs on the platform
used to compile your executable.

- `zml.Buffer.fromSlice(io, platform, slice)`\
  `!Buffer`. Copy a `zml.Slice` from host memory and wait for transfer.
- `zml.Buffer.fromBytes(io, platform, shape, bytes)`\
  `!Buffer`. Copy host bytes using the supplied `Shape`. The bytes must match its element type and size.
- `buffer.shape()`\
  `Shape`. Read the buffer's metadata.
- `buffer.await(io)`\
  `!void`. Wait for the backend buffer to become ready.
- `buffer.getValue(T, io)`\
  `!T`. Read back into a host type with matching representation and byte size. It copies bytes without converting the element type.
- `buffer.toSliceAlloc(allocator, io)`\
  `!Slice`. Read back into an owned host slice. Release it with `slice.free(allocator)`.
- `buffer.deinit()`\
  `void`. Release the buffer through a mutable handle.

When using `fromSliceOpts` or `fromBytesOpts` with `.wait = false`, keep the
source bytes alive and unchanged until `buffer.await(io)` completes successfully.

# [Platform]($heading.id('platform'))

[`zml.Platform`](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/platform.zig)
holds the backend runtime and devices. The initializer options are
`zml.platform.CreateOptions`. The compile calls take `zml.Compiler.Options`.

- `zml.Platform.auto(allocator, io, options)`\
  `!*Platform`. Try backends in priority order and return the first one that initializes. Return `error.Unavailable` if none initialize.
- `zml.Platform.init(allocator, io, target, options)`\
  `!*Platform`. Initialize a specific `zml.Target`, such as `.cpu` or `.cuda`, when present in the build.
- `platform.compile(allocator, io, model, .forward, .{input}, options)`\
  `!Exe`. Trace the named method of `model`. Pass the remaining method arguments in a tuple, as with `input` here.
- `platform.compileFn(allocator, io, function, args, options)`\
  `!Exe`. Trace a function with the full tuple of its arguments.
- `platform.deinit(allocator, io)`\
  `void`. Release the platform after its buffers and executables have been released.

See [Build and run with Bazel]($link.page('zml/build-and-run')) for backend flags
and the selection order used by `auto`.

# [Exe]($heading.id('exe'))

[`zml.Exe`](https://github.com/zml/zml/blob/8fcff8c462070d06056f8a85ba0c8a81f0f3a636/zml/exe.zig)
is the loaded executable returned by compilation. Pass buffers in the structure
and order of the compiled arguments, using the expected sizes, types, and shards.

- `exe.args(allocator)`\
  `!Exe.Arguments`. Allocate argument slots for the executable.
- `arguments.set(buffers)`\
  `void`. Bind the provided buffers to the argument slots.
- `exe.results(allocator)`\
  `!Exe.Results`. Allocate result slots for the executable.
- `exe.call(io, arguments, &results, .{ .wait = true })`\
  `void`. Run with the bound buffers and fill the result slots. `wait` defaults to `false` when omitted.
- `results.get(zml.Buffer)`\
  `Buffer`. Retrieve the output of a single-output call. For multiple outputs, pass the matching structure of buffers.
- `exe.eval(allocator, io, buffers)`\
  `!Buffer`. Run a single-output executable, wait, and return an owned result buffer.
- `exe.deinit()`\
  `void`. Release the loaded executable.

Release argument and result slots with `deinit(allocator)`. Retrieve each
output once per execution and release each owned buffer with `buffer.deinit()`.
When the compiled computation donates an input, execution invalidates its
previous contents. A raw `call` leaves cleanup of its input handles to the
caller. `eval` consumes donated handles.

